43. The key architectural insight

I think we've now nailed something important about MOS:

MOS doesn't need to know every tool. It needs to know how to discover, evaluate, select, use, remember, and learn from capabilities.

MCP gives us the standardized capability interface.

PostgreSQL gives us durable truth and provenance.

TerminusDB gives us relationship traversal and graph history.

pgvector gives us semantic retrieval.

Redis gives us speed and runtime state.

And MOS's cognitive layer determines:

"What am I trying to accomplish?"
        ↓
"What information do I need?"
        ↓
"What capabilities are available?"
        ↓
"Which capability is best?"
        ↓
"Am I allowed to use it?"
        ↓
"Did it work?"
        ↓
"What did I learn?"
        ↓
"Should I change my strategy?"

That is exactly the kind of closed-loop system we've been building toward.

I would now consider the MCP boundary defined enough to proceed to the updated MOS Cache Specification v0.1. The cache spec should explicitly incorporate the MCP capability cache, resource cache, tool-result cache, host-context cache, and learned tool-selection statistics we have defined here.
